1. public/
This folder contains everything ordinary website visitors can access.
public stores the customer-facing website.


2. src/
This is the main Node.js backend folder.
src stores the server-side application code.


src/config/
config stores setup and connection settings.
supabase.js will connect Node.js to Supabase.


src/routes/
Routes define the URLs that the server accepts.


src/controllers/
Controllers contain the actual logic executed by routes.
The route receives the request, then calls the controller to decide what the server should do with a request.




src/middleware/
Middleware runs between the request and the final controller.
Middleware sits between Express and the response.
    Check authentication
    Validate data
    errorHandler
we want to reject any booking where:
    Name is empty
    Passengers < 1
    Date is in the past
Why middleware often uses exports directly e.g exports.logger = ()=>{...}
    A middleware file often contains only one middleware.
Then why do we import with { }? 
    What gets exported is actually an object. { logger: logger }
    then const { logger } = require(objectUrl);




src/services/
Services contain reusable business logic.
Services perform reusable work that controllers need preventung controllers from becoming too large.
    Suppose later we want the home page to show:
        Featured vehicles
        Three latest testimonials
        Company statistics
    Question: Which layer should retrieve that information?




src/utils/
This contains small helper functions.
    Format telephone numbers
    Format dates



app.use()
I'm delegating this responsibility to another module.



